# mobius_band.pwmodel - a solid Mobius band: a 108 x 6 rectangular section swept
# once around a 180-radius circle while the section itself turns a half turn, so
# the two flat faces of the band are ONE face that goes round twice.
#
# THIS IS THE MACHINE-GENERATED TIER, AND IT IS THE ONLY EXAMPLE IN THIS FOLDER
# THAT USES IT.
# Every other file here is meant to be edited a line at a time: change
# `radius=18` and you have moved a hole. Nothing below works that way. The part
# opens with `procedural_mesh` - an explicitly empty mesh - fills it from one
# `append_buffers` line of 400 positions and 800 index triples, and then edits
# that mesh BY INDEX: `delete_triangle index=182`, `set_vertex_position
# index=91`. A human must not type those numbers, and an LLM asked to "make the
# hole bigger" must not edit them either. THERE IS NO GENERATOR TO RE-RUN - the
# construction lives in this header instead, under THE CONSTRUCTION below, which
# carries every formula the 400 positions came out of. Regenerate the whole part
# from it ad hoc, run the seam tests, and replace the table in one go.
#
# WHY THE REST OF THE FORMAT CARRIES NO INDICES AT ALL
# An index is a reference to a piece of geometry some earlier op produced, and
# `.pwmodel` stores no such reference anywhere else - not to a face, an edge, a
# vertex or a triangle - deliberately. `extrude` picks faces with
# `face_direction`, a PREDICATE, which survives its input changing shape. An
# index does not. Add one station to the sweep below and every triangle id after
# it slides by 20; `delete_triangle index=182` then cuts a hole somewhere else,
# `set_vertex_position index=91` moves the wrong vertex, and NOTHING FAILS - the
# document still compiles, to a different and silently wrong mesh. That is the
# face-identity problem the format avoids by not having identities. This tier is
# the deliberate exception, and its contract is: the buffer and the indices that
# address it are one artifact, regenerated together or not at all.
#
# WHY THIS SHAPE EARNS THE EXCEPTION - AND THE HALF OF THAT CLAIM THAT WAS FALSE
# This section used to end "the one construction the op vocabulary cannot
# express". That is WRONG, and it was wrong in the direction that keeps 400
# hand-listed positions alive for no reason. `sweep` and `extrude_along_spline`
# BOTH take `twist` - "total twist in degrees, applied linearly from the first
# frame to the last" - which is precisely a profile frame that turns as it goes.
# `torus` and the `twist` DEFORMER are still no use, for the reasons given; the
# error was stopping there and not reading the sweep ops' own parameter list.
#
# What survives is narrower, and it is about CLOSING THE LOOP, not about twisting.
# Measured 2026-08-21 on UE 5.8:
#
#   `sweep` never closes a path - it sweeps open and caps, always. N frames of a
#   4-point profile give V = 4N and T = 8*(N-1) + 4. The obvious ring - 9 frames
#   round a 180 circle, the last repeating the first - gives V=36 T=68, so Euler
#   V - E + F = 36 - 102 + 68 = 2. That is a topological BALL: a twisted bar bent
#   round until its two capped ends touch, both lids still in the mesh, one
#   inside the other. It reports `isClosed: true`, `componentCount: 1`,
#   `orientationConsistent: true`, positive volume and no diagnostics. Nothing in
#   a health block says "this is not a band".
#
#   `extrude_along_spline` DOES close - when the last frame returns within
#   0.01 uu of the first - and it takes `twist` as well. 40 frames round the same
#   circle at `twist=180` give V=160 T=320, Euler 160 - 480 + 320 = 0, a torus,
#   and it passes the CUT test below: one piece at a half twist, two at none and
#   two at a full turn. It is a genuine Mobius band, in one line.
#
# The twist also reaches its full value on a closed path - the `scale_end` defect
# that drops the last frame does NOT bite here. Two measurements: the band's peak
# |z| lands between 54 and 56, against hypot(54, 3) = 54.083 for a section that
# stands exactly upright; and the solid's volume excess over the ideal 108*6*L
# falls as 1/N (148573 at N=40, 77483 at N=80, 39507 at N=160), i.e. it converges
# to the true swept solid instead of holding the constant offset a mismatched
# seam would leave.
#
# So a one-line sweep reproduces this SURFACE. What it does not reproduce is the
# APERTURE: `delete_triangle index=`, `delete_vertex index=` and
# `set_vertex_position index=` address the buffer BY ID, and
# `docs/pwmodel-format.md` lists "vertex and triangle ORDER" under "Not
# guaranteed, and do not assume". An explicit table is what makes an id mean
# anything. That - not the twist - is what this tier actually buys, and whether a
# hexagonal slot is worth 400 typed positions is a corpus question, not a
# geometry one. It is open; nothing here has been rebuilt on the sweep.
#
# THE PROPORTION IS THE DESIGN, AND THE OBVIOUS FIX WAS THE WRONG WAY ROUND
# 108 wide on a 180 mean radius: a width-to-radius ratio of 0.60. That number is
# the whole redesign, and it was reached by rendering seven shapes across five
# ratios at one pinned three-quarter (azimuth 90, elevation 28, `ev100: -1`)
# rather than by picking it. Every row below is a render that was looked at.
#
#   0.42 x 16 thick   the shipped shape - reads as a bowl rim or a wide bangle
#   0.27 x  8         a bangle. Narrower is worse, not better
#   0.27 x  3         still a bangle. Thinning does not rescue a narrow band
#   0.42 x  4         better - a ribbon, but the ring still reads as a dish
#   0.56 x  5         reads
#   0.60 x  6         reads - what ships
#   0.83 x  6         a scoop. The flat half swallows the hole
#
# The standing advice was to NARROW the strip so its two legs separate where they
# cross. Measured, that makes it worse at every width tried, and the reason is
# worth keeping: the only visible evidence of a half twist is the band's own
# surface rotating from flat to vertical, and narrowing is precisely what removes
# the surface that carries it. A narrow twisted ring has nothing left to read but
# its rim, and a rim is a bangle. What DOES read is a wide, thin ribbon: at 0.60
# the band stands as a tall wall at theta 180 and lies as a broad sheet at theta
# 0, and one frame contains both. Thickness matters too but second - 6 on 108 is
# 18:1, and the 16-on-76 original read as masonry rather than as a ribbon.
#
# There is a ceiling as well as a floor. Past about 0.7 the flat half of the band
# covers the hole and the model reads as a scoop, so the window is roughly
# 0.55-0.67 and the middle of it is what ships.
#
# THE CONSTRUCTION - EVERY POSITION IN THIS FILE COMES OUT OF THESE LINES
# The text is the source, so the construction has to live here rather than in a
# script next to it. Angles in radians.
#
#   R = 180                    mean radius of the ring
#   station s = 0 .. 39        theta = 2*pi*s / 40      (9 degrees apart)
#   perimeter p = 0 .. 9       section point (a, b), a across the band,
#                              b through its thickness:
#                                p <= 4:  a = -54 + 27*p,        b = +3
#                                p >= 5:  a = +54 - 27*(p - 5),  b = -3
#                              so the section is the 108 x 6 rectangle, walked
#                              out along the top face and back along the bottom.
#   phi = theta / 2            THE HALF TWIST. The section turns once for every
#                              two turns of theta, so a full trip round the ring
#                              rotates it by pi.
#
#   surface(theta, a, b):
#       u_r = a*cos(phi) - b*sin(phi)      offset along the outward radial
#       u_z = a*sin(phi) + b*cos(phi)      offset along +Z
#       x   = (R + u_r) * cos(theta)
#       y   = (R + u_r) * sin(theta)
#       z   = u_z
#
# vertex(s, p) = surface(2*pi*s/40, a(p), b(p)), emitted station-major, printed
# to 3 decimals with trailing zeros and the point stripped - "126", not
# "126.000". 11 of the 1200 coordinates round to a negative zero; they are
# written "0".
#
# Quad (s, p) joins station s to station s+1 and is split on the s -> s+1
# diagonal, which is the detail that decides the aperture's shape further down:
#       A = v(s, p)   B = v(s+1, p)   C = v(s+1, p+1)   D = v(s, p+1)
#       triangle (s, p, 0) = (A, B, C)      triangle (s, p, 1) = (A, C, D)
# That order is what puts (v1-v0) x (v2-v0) INWARD on all 800 - see the note
# above the buffer for why inward is the visible direction.
#
# THE SEAM. Station 40 IS station 0, but rotated: phi(2*pi) = pi carries the
# section onto itself through (a, b) -> (-a, -b), and on this numbering that is
# perimeter p -> (p + 5) mod 10. So the last ring of quads reads
#       v(40, p) = v(0, (p + 5) mod 10)
# and that one substitution, in ten quads, is the entire Mobius. Everything above
# about proving it applies to this line and nothing else.
#
# The aperture rim targets come off the same function, not off a chord:
#       target = surface(pi/2 + du/R, a, +/-3)
# with pi/2 being station 10 and (du, a) walking the hexagon (+30, 0) (+15, +16)
# (-15, +16) (-30, 0) (-15, -16) (+15, -16). The collision slabs are that
# function again: slab k is the hull of surface() at stations 4k, 4k+2 and 4k+4
# crossed with perimeter 0, 4, 5, 9 - the four corner rails, three stations,
# twelve points, ten slabs of 36 degrees.
#
# Reproduced from these formulae alone on 2026-08-21 and checked against the
# shipped text: all 400 positions, all 800 index triples, all 12
# `set_vertex_position` targets, all 36 bore-wall coordinates and all 10
# collision elements match exactly as written.
#
# HOW TO READ THE INDICES
# The sweep is 40 stations around the ring, 10 points around the section:
#
#     vertex id   = station * 10 + perimeter
#     triangle id = (station * 10 + perimeter) * 2 + (0 | 1)
#
# Perimeter 0..4 walk the top face (b = +3) from the inner rail to the outer,
# 5..9 walk the bottom face (b = -3) back. So perimeter 0/4/5/9 are the four
# corner rails and 4->5 and 9->0 are the two narrow edge faces. Vertex 91 is
# station 9, perimeter 1. Triangle 182 is the first half of the quad at station
# 9, perimeter 1. This mapping is a property of the emission order and is the
# only thing that makes the file re-readable; it is also exactly what breaks if
# the station count changes.
#
# Triangle ids being the emission order is MEASURED, not promised:
# `docs/pwmodel-format.md` lists "vertex and triangle ORDER" under "Not
# guaranteed, and do not assume". A probe appended two disjoint quads, deleted
# ids 2 and 3, and got the second quad back - so on UE 5.8 ids are handed out in
# supply order for a fresh `procedural_mesh`. Anything built on that has to
# re-measure, which is the second half of why this tier is fenced off.
#
# THE HALF TWIST IS A PERMUTATION IN THE TRIANGLE LIST, NOT A SHAPE OP
# Stations 0..38 wrap the obvious way: quad (i, p) joins station i to station
# i+1 at the same perimeter index. The LAST ring is where the Mobius is: after a
# half turn the section has mapped onto itself rotated by half its perimeter, so
# station 39 joins station 0 at perimeter (p + 5) mod 10, not at p. Ten quads
# with a +5 offset, buried in the middle of the index list, are the entire
# topological content of this model. That is worth saying out loud, because it
# is invisible: the mesh is closed and manifold either way, and closing it with
# the naive p -> p correspondence produces a self-crossing tangle that still
# compiles green.
#
# The positions cannot prove it either - the sweep formula carries the half turn
# whatever the seam says, so both seams emit the SAME 400 positions. Three tests
# do prove it. Run all three against any regenerated table:
#
#   GEOMETRIC   the point the seam joins perimeter p to must be surface(2*pi, a,
#               b) for that p's section. Mobius: exact, to the last printed
#               decimal. Naive: off by 108.167 - which is the section DIAGONAL,
#               hypot(108, 6), not the band width of 108 this line used to name.
#   TOPOLOGICAL walk the face strips across the seam. Strip p returns to strip
#               (p + 5) mod 10, so every orbit has size 2 - one face that goes
#               round twice, which is what "Mobius" means. Naive: every orbit
#               has size 1, i.e. two faces, i.e. an ordinary annulus. Walked on
#               the triangles instead - merging across every edge softer than
#               the 40 degree split angle used below - the same test reads 2
#               smooth regions, 640 triangles of face and 160 of rim, against
#               the naive seam's 8.
#   CUT         the classic one, and the only one that needs no index scheme at
#               all: subtract a thin slab down the middle of the section, the
#               whole way round, and count components. A Mobius band falls into
#               ONE piece; an annulus falls into two. Measured on the swept
#               surface: half twist 1, no twist 2, full turn 2.
#
# WINDING IS NOT ONE OF THE TESTS, though it is the one that looks like it. A
# naive seam trips the inward-normal assert on 4 of 800 triangles at this width,
# on 2 at 76 wide, and on NONE at 60 wide or under. A green winding check is
# therefore not evidence the seam is right - it is evidence the band is narrow.
#
# NO `uvs=` ON THE BUFFER, DELIBERATELY
# The bore wall further down is 12 `append_triangle` ops, and `append_triangle`
# carries no UVs. Supplying `uvs=` on `append_buffers` would therefore leave UV
# channel 0 SET on 788 triangles and UNSET on 12 - the partly-mapped state that
# `docs/pwmodel-format.md` documents as fatal to `shell` and `bevel`, and which
# the compiler's own box-projection fill will not rescue because it skips any
# channel that already carries elements. So the buffer ships with no UVs at all
# and a single `uv mode=cylindrical` at the end of the part maps everything,
# appended triangles included.
#
# THE APERTURE IS THE TRIANGLE STAR OF ONE VERTEX, CUT TWICE, TWO WAYS
# A hexagonal slot passes clean through the band at station 10, where the
# section stands at 45 degrees and the twist reads most clearly. The opening on
# each face is the triangle star of a single interior grid vertex - 102 on the
# top face, 107 on the bottom - and the two are cut with different ops on
# purpose. They produce identical openings; the contrast is the lesson:
#
#   top    `delete_triangle` x 6 - the star listed as six triangle ids.
#   bottom `delete_vertex`   x 1 - the vertex. The op removes it AND every
#                                  triangle using it, which is the same six.
#
# `delete_vertex` is shorter and re-derives its own extent, so it survives a
# change of grid density that would leave six literal ids pointing at whatever
# now sits at 182..205. `delete_triangle` is the only one of the two that can
# remove a triangle no vertex uniquely owns. Neither is the right one; the file
# shows both because the choice is real and the equivalence is not obvious.
#
# THE STAR IS SIX TRIANGLES, NOT EIGHT - MEASURED, AND IT DECIDES THE SHAPE
# The intuition that an interior vertex of a quad grid owns four quads, and so
# eight triangles, is wrong once the quads are triangulated. Every quad here is
# split on the same A-C diagonal, so of the four quads touching a vertex, two
# contribute both their triangles and two contribute one each: 6, and a one-ring
# of six vertices, not eight. The first cut of this model was written for eight
# and compiled green at 802 triangles with 6 boundary edges - a bore wall built
# for an octagon stitched onto a hexagonal hole, which no count in the compile
# response calls an error. `health.boundaryEdges` is what caught it.
#
# The one-ring is also SHEARED, for the same reason: at (station 10, perimeter
# 2) it runs (+28,0) (+28,+27) (0,+27) (-28,0) (-28,-27) (0,-27) in
# (arc offset, across-band offset). That is a parallelogram-ish hexagon whose
# two long sides are staircases. It is the diagonal's shape, not a shape anyone
# chose, and it is why the next section exists.
#
# SHAPING THE OPENING - WHAT `set_vertex_position` IS ACTUALLY FOR
# Twelve `set_vertex_position` ops move the six rim vertices, on each face, from
# that sheared hexagon onto a symmetric one 60 long and 32 wide: ends at
# du = +/-30, corners at (+/-15, +/-16). The slot then runs WITH the band and
# leaves a 38-wide rail down each side.
#
# The alternative was to re-run the generator with a station count and a section
# subdivision whose grid happens to land on those numbers - design by divisor,
# moving all 400 vertices to fix six. Moving six named vertices is the honest
# fix and it is only expressible at this tier. Every target is still evaluated
# ON the swept surface, surface(u + du/R, a, +/-3), so the rim stays on the band
# instead of being nudged along a chord. At this radius the arc-to-chord
# deviation is 0.6 uu at the +/-15 corners and 2.5 uu at the +/-30 ends. That is
# not a rounding error on a band only 6 thick - half-thickness is 3, so a chord
# would carry the two end points most of the way to the opposite face.
#
# The two rims run in OPPOSITE directions. The bottom face's perimeter
# numbering increases the other way, so its one-ring, walked in the same order,
# traces the hexagon backwards: rim vertex k on the bottom is sent to hexagon
# corner (6 - k) mod 6. Get that wrong and the twelve moves still compile, still
# report 800 triangles, and braid the bore into a hexagram.
#
# THE BORE WALL - `append_triangle` TAKES POSITIONS, NOT INDICES
# Twelve `append_triangle` ops wall the slot from the top rim to the bottom rim.
# Note the signature: v0=, v1=, v2=, three POSITIONS. No op anywhere in
# `pwmodel 0` builds a triangle out of vertex ids, which is why the wall repeats
# all 12 rim positions instead of referencing them, and why `weld_vertices` has
# to run afterwards to stitch 36 duplicate vertices back onto the rims. The
# coordinates below are the POST-`set_vertex_position` ones; retune the hexagon
# without regenerating them and the weld quietly finds no pairs, leaving a
# hairline crack round both rims that no count in the compile response reports.
#
# This is also the only honest use of `append_triangle` on a model like this.
# Bulk geometry belongs in `append_buffers`: one line, local indices, no
# duplication. `append_triangle` is the patch op - a handful of triangles laid
# against geometry that already exists.
#
# THE WARNINGS THAT ARE NO LONGER THERE
# This file used to compile with nine `PWMODEL_UNUNIONED_OVERLAP` warnings, one
# per `append_triangle` overlapping another: the check compares sibling bounding
# boxes, and the boxes of two triangles that share an edge always interpenetrate,
# while its advice - wrap the second op in a union block - cannot apply to a
# triangle, which encloses no volume.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-21, on this geometry: `diagnosticSummary.total: 0`. The check
# runs only from the generator path and `append_triangle` is not on it, the same
# reason it can never fire on `mirror` or `array_*`. Do not add ops here
# expecting to be warned about an overlap - on this file nothing will warn you,
# and `health.componentCount` plus a render are the signals that remain.
#
# COLLISION IS 10 CONVEX SLABS, BECAUSE A RING HULLS TO A DISC
# One hull round this model is a disc 468 across with the hole filled in - the
# characteristic failure of convex collision on any closed loop. So the ring is
# decomposed into ten 36-degree slabs, each the convex hull of the four corner
# rails sampled at three stations. Twelve points per element, all computed. A
# slab still over-covers slightly, because the section rotates 18 degrees across
# the wedge and a hull cannot follow that; `complexity = simple_and_complex`
# keeps an exact complex mesh alongside for anything that needs one.
#
# `convex points=[...]` is not the authoring path and the format reference says
# so in those words. Element 0 is written the other way, as
# `hull { procedural_mesh + append_vertex x 12 }`, to show what the compact form
# compresses: a `hull` block runs its body through the ordinary op pipeline and
# then keeps only the VERTEX POSITIONS, so a body of bare `append_vertex` ops
# with no triangles at all is a legal point cloud and hulls correctly. That is
# the one place a bare `append_vertex` means anything - inside a part it would
# add a vertex no triangle could ever reference, since no op builds a triangle
# from ids. The other nine elements are the same data in the form a generator
# emits, and they are the ones to copy.

pwmodel 0

# THE SLOT IS BOUND BY ITS SYNTHETIC NAME, AND NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS TAGGED.
# That combination is deliberate and it is the only one that gets this model a
# colour without giving up the measurement below.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT. `append_buffers` takes `material="<Slot>"` like any generator,
# but `append_triangle` takes neither `material=` nor `color=`. Binding the body
# to a named slot therefore does NOT carry the bore wall with it. Compiled with
# `materials { Cast = "/Engine/BasicShapes/BasicShapeMaterial" }` and
# `material="Cast"` on both `procedural_mesh` and `append_buffers`, this model
# reports `materialSlots: 2`, and `static_mesh.describe` names them:
#
#   [ { slot: "Cast",    path: ".../BasicShapeMaterial" },
#     { slot: "Default", path: ".../WorldGridMaterial"  } ]
#
# The twelve appended wall triangles went to a synthetic "Default" slot - not to
# slot index 0, which is what "raw material id, an index into the model-wide
# slot list in first-use order" would predict - so the inside of the aperture
# rendered in the grid material while the band rendered in the bound one. Naming
# the slot on `procedural_mesh` as well does not change it. Do not do that.
#
# WHAT THE FILE DOES INSTEAD. `Default` is the slot the compiler invents for
# UNTAGGED geometry, and it turns out to be an ordinary name that a `materials`
# block can bind. Tag nothing, bind `Default`, and every triangle in the model -
# swept, appended, welded - lands in one slot with one material. Verified:
# `materialSlots: 1`, no diagnostics, `static_mesh.describe` names the single
# slot `Default`.
#
# THE COLOUR IS ONE VALUE FOR THE WHOLE BAND, AND THAT IS THE SAME LIMITATION
# SEEN FROM THE INSIDE. `append_triangle` takes no `color=` either, so the bore
# wall cannot be tinted apart from the surface it is cut through even now:
# `set_vertex_color set_all=true` is all-or-nothing by construction. A model that
# needed a two-tone aperture would have to build the wall with `append_buffers`
# and give up single-triangle placement - which is exactly the trade the
# paragraph above names, arrived at from the colour side instead of the slot side.
#
# `set_all` is safe in this part because there is NO BOOLEAN in it. The op does
# not reach geometry a boolean has contributed as a tool (measured; see the note
# in amphora.pwmodel), and every op here - delete, reposition, append, weld - acts
# on the one accumulating mesh.
materials {
    Default = "/Game/PinWrightExamples/Materials/MI_PwModelExample_MatteDielectric"
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SWEPT SURFACE
# 400 vertices = 40 stations x 10 perimeter points, emitted station-major.
# 800 triangles = 40 wrap rings x 10 quads x 2, emitted in the same order, so
# triangle (station, perimeter, half) is at (station * 10 + perimeter) * 2 + half.
# The mesh is closed and manifold as it stands - the boolean-free, cut-free,
# fully generated form of the model. Everything after this line is surgery.
#
# Winding: Unreal's visible face is the NEGATION of the right-hand-rule normal,
# so every triangle here is wound so that (v1-v0) x (v2-v0) points INWARD, at
# the centreline. The generator asserts that on all 800 before emitting; get it
# backwards and the band renders as a black hole with a lit interior.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
part band {
